Symptom 1

ERROR: workspace .../nightly-build is already locked

Root cause: Two builds of the same job are running concurrently (overlapping executions, a
previous run still finishing, or a manual + scheduled trigger colliding), and Jenkins serializes access
to a workspace shared by job name.

Fix: If concurrency wasn't intended, check "Do not allow concurrent builds" in the job's General
settings to serialize execution cleanly. If concurrency is intended, instead configure a custom

workspace naming strategy (e.g.
${JOB_NAME}-${EXECUTOR_NUMBER}
) 
so each concurrent executor gets its own isolated workspace rather than sharing one directory.


Symptom 2

Permission denied: cannot write to /var/jenkins_home/workspace/nightly-build/
build.log

Root cause: The build is intermittently picked up by a different agent/executor running under a
different OS user context (or a prior build left files owned by root/another UID after a

sudo
step), so the
jenkins
user lacks write permission on files left behind.

Fix: Pin the job to a single specific agent/label ("Restrict where this project can run" → fixed node
label) so it always runs under the same user context, and add a "Delete workspace before build
starts" (Wipe Workspace) build step so no stale, wrongly-owned files remain.


Symptom 3

Occasionally the build uses a stale copy of the repo, missing the latest
commit.

Root cause: The Git checkout is reusing a cached local
.git
state without cleaning, so under load a build occasionally starts before the fetch/poll has picked up
the newest ref.

Fix: In the Git SCM configuration, add the "Clean before checkout" and "Clean after checkout"
Additional Behaviours to wipe stale refs, confirm the refspec is the full

+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*

(not a narrow/cached ref), and disable "Force polling using workspace" so SCM polling always
queries the remote directly instead of trusting a potentially stale local state.
